  "interpretation": "Both 10\u00d7 scenes devote about 30 ms/frame to HAPFS and 8 ms/frame to ground movement on each backend. Grid searches account for about 21 ms and lower-resolution estimator searches for about 8 ms within HAPFS. Native path searches are the largest identified shared cost on this one-worker fixture. Normal fire had more CPU fence time in Rust in the saved pair. The original rendering gap remains open.",
  "source_status": "baseline",
  "path_method": {
    "grouping": "Each main-thread self weight goes to HAPFS manager Update, GroundMoveSystem Update outside HAPFS, or remaining CPU. Within HAPFS it goes to grid search, estimator search, or remaining path updates, with grid taking precedence on nested stacks. Footprint and exit-only totals are nested subsets.",
    "source": "https://github.com/gajop/spring-rust/blob/782c0e8a6837ec0b8ecf5f38a4d4815debc8119a/rts/Sim/Path/HAPFS/PathManager.cpp#L779",
    "footprint_source": "https://github.com/gajop/spring-rust/blob/782c0e8a6837ec0b8ecf5f38a4d4815debc8119a/rts/Sim/MoveTypes/MoveMath/MoveMath.cpp#L155",
    "opportunity": "The footprint function constructs a collision query with an exit-only scan, then checks objects through ObjectBlockType and IsNonBlocking, which do not read that flag. The pathfinder separately checks exit-only state when expanding a node. A narrower collision query could skip the constructor scan, while preserving the independent traversal checks. This has not been tested as an engine change."
  },
